<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nazrin Shah of Perak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. 1956</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Nazrin Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nazrin Shah is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Perak, Malaysia, who succeeded his father Sultan Azlan Shah in 2014. Before his accession he served for many years as Raja Muda, the heir to the throne, and he holds a doctorate in political economy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>. As one of Malaysia's nine hereditary rulers he takes part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-rulers">Conference of Rulers</a>, which elects the country's king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nazrin-shah-of-perak.jpg" alt="Nazrin Shah of Perak" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iclif Executive Education Centre at ASB · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nazrin_Shah_of_Perak_in_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
